<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Dreux</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Dreux was a cadet branch of the French royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">Capetian dynasty</a>, descended from Robert I, Count of Dreux, a son of Louis VI. In 1213 Philip II married the Breton heiress Alix to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-i-duke-of-brittany">Peter Mauclerc</a> of Dreux, whose descendants, including the later Montfort dukes, ruled the Duchy of Brittany until the line ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-of-brittany">Anne of Brittany</a> in 1514.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-dreux.jpg" alt="House of Dreux" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlodangio · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_Robert_de_Dreux.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The house was founded by Robert I, Count of Dreux, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vi-of-france">Louis VI of France</a>, who received the County of Dreux as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a>. The counts of Dreux remained relatively minor nobles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and their senior comital line died out in 1345. Through their kinship with the counts of Vermandois, themselves of Capetian descent, they bore a chessboard of gold and azure, which in the 13th century marked their cousinship with the kings of France.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The family's rise came when the French king, needing an obedient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> to marry Alix, Duchess of Brittany, turned to his cousin Peter, a younger son of the Count of Dreux. Called Mauclerc, the bad cleric, Peter had at first been devoted to the clergy, and on renouncing it he kept the quarter of ermine that marked such princes. His marriage placed the house in one of the most important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Brittany became a lay peerage of France in 1297 and was formally recognised as a duchy by the French court.</p><p>When John III, Duke of Brittany died in 1341, his niece <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-penthievre">Joan of Penthièvre</a> and his younger half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-montfort">John of Montfort</a> both claimed the duchy. Joan's husband, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-of-blois">Charles of Blois</a>, was a nephew of King Philip VI, who backed them, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a> supported the Montforts. John of Montfort died in 1345, and Charles was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-auray">Battle of Auray</a> in 1364 fighting John's son. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerande">Guérande</a>, which ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-breton-succession">War of the Breton Succession</a>, made the duchy hereditary in the male Montfort line, with the males of Blois-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penthievre">Penthièvre</a> after them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penthievre">Penthièvre</a> family imprisoned the duke in 1420 in a failed attempt to regain Brittany. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a>, the last male Montfort duke, died, the duchy passed to his daughter, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-of-brittany">Anne of Brittany</a>. The house also left Brittany its arms. In 1316 John III gave up the Dreux chessboard with its ermine quarter, which marked a cadet line and made the duchy a heraldic dependency of the small county of Dreux, and adopted plain ermine, partly because his stepmother Yolande de Dreux bore the same arms as he did.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Dreux” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
